   427      // THE MINT: the inception, then the key file, and THE KEY FILE COMMITS THE MINT. Both land inside ONE hold of the
   428      // custody claim (`exclusiveCustody`), the same claim every sealed write in every process takes, so two racing acts
   429      // never interleave: the first lands both files, and the later act meets the key that stands, writes nothing and
   430      // answers that key. A mint runs only inside a held custody and refuses by name outside one, before any byte lands:
   431      // a founding seals its custody first (`lares vessel found`), so the place's key mints inside the act that holds
   432      // its custody open. A key file that stands always stands beside the inception that names it; an act that stops
   433      // between the two leaves an inception no key answers to, and the next mint writes it afresh.
